<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Margaret of Parma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1522">1522</a>–<a href="/years/1586">1586</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Margaret of Parma was an illegitimate daughter of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. Married first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-de-medici">Alessandro de' Medici</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottavio-farnese">Ottavio Farnese</a>, duke of Parma, she was governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> for her half-brother Philip II from 1559 to 1567. Her rule saw growing opposition to Spanish religious policy, and she resigned after the arrival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a>. Her son was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-farnese-duke-of-parma">Alessandro Farnese</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/margaret-of-parma.jpg" alt="Margaret of Parma" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antonis Mor · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anthonis_Mor_-_Margaret,_Duchess_of_Parma_-_WGA16182.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Margaret was born on 5 July 1522, the illegitimate daughter of the Holy Roman Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> and Johanna Maria van der Gheynst, a servant of Count Charles de Lalaing. She was brought up at Mechelen under her great-aunt Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and her aunt Mary of Austria, successive governors of the Habsburg Netherlands. Charles used her to secure his plans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>: she was engaged in 1527 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-de-medici">Alessandro de' Medici, Duke of Florence</a>, a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii">Pope Clement VII</a>, and from 1533 she was educated in the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and Parma. Though multilingual, she preferred Italian for the rest of her life.</p><p>She married Alessandro on 13 June 1536, and he was assassinated on 6 January 1537. On 4 November 1538 the 15-year-old widow married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottavio-farnese">Ottavio Farnese</a>, Duke of Parma, who was 14. She at first refused. The marriage was unhappy and the couple lived apart for much of their lives, but it produced twin sons, one of whom died in infancy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1555 Margaret left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, leaving her son in the care of her half-brother Philip II, and in 1559, when Philip left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, he appointed her Governor of the Habsburg Netherlands. Her authority was largely nominal. She faced growing discontent against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> and Spanish rule and had to follow the advice of Cardinal Granvelle, who was much disliked and was exiled in 1564.</p><p>In 1565 an opposition party formed among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> nobility, and with no army to put it down she promised to stop religious repression. In 1566 she quelled iconoclastic riots with the help of the stadtholders Philip of Noircarmes and William of Orange. Philip then sent the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a> with an army, and Margaret, warning that his actions would lead to catastrophe, resigned in 1567 when she learned that Alba's power of attorney superseded her own.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Margaret retired in 1567 to L'Aquila in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and was appointed Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a>. In 1578 her son Alexander Farnese became governor-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and Philip made her co-regent, but the two could not work together and she retired to Namur in 1582. She returned to Italy with Philip's permission in 1583, died at Ortona on 18 January 1586 and was buried at S. Sisto in Piacenza. Charlie R. Steen describes her as a woman dedicated to compromise and conciliation in public affairs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Margaret of Parma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Ottavio Farnese held on to Parma, with French support in the War of Parma of 1551–1552, and through his marriage to <b>Margaret of Parma</b>, the illegitimate daughter of Charles V, he reconciled the family with the Habsburgs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Two hundred noblemen led by Hendrick van Brederode forced their way to the regent <b>Margaret of Parma</b> with a petition seeking to moderate the placards against heresy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-5">5 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Oudenaarde, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. Died in Ortona, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.85,3.6&amp;year=1563" aria-label="Oudenaarde on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4632 400 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Margaret of Parma"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5082 670h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5082 670h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.85,3.6&amp;year=1563">What was here in 1563? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
